.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt SEQ 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm seq
.Nd print a sequence of numbers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm seq
.Op Fl w
.Op Fl f Ar format
.Op Fl s Ar string
.Op Ar first Op Ar increment
.Ar last
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility prints a sequence of numbers, one per line (by default), from
.Ar first
(default 1), to near
.Ar last
as possible, in increments of
.Ar increment
(default 1).
When
.Ar first
is larger than
.Ar last ,
the default
.Ar increment
is still 1, so no output is produced unless a negative
.Ar increment
is specified.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f Ar format , Fl Fl format Ns = Ns Ar format
Use a
.Xr printf 1
style floating-point
.Ar format
for printing each number.
Supported conversion specifiers are
.Cm %f
(fixed-point),
.Cm %e
(scientific notation), and
.Cm %g
(general).
The default format is auto-detected from the input precision.
.It Fl s Ar string , Fl Fl separator Ns = Ns Ar string
Use
.Ar string
to separate numbers.
The default separator is a newline character.
.It Fl w , Fl Fl equal-width
Equalize the widths of all numbers by padding with leading zeroes
as necessary.
The width is determined by the widest of
.Ar first
and
.Ar last .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std seq
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print 1 through 5:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ seq 5
1
2
3
4
5
.Ed
.Pp
Print 3 through 7:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ seq 3 7
3
4
5
6
7
.Ed
.Pp
Print even numbers from 0 to 10:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ seq 0 2 10
0
2
4
6
8
10
.Ed
.Pp
Count down from 5 to 1:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ seq 5 -1 1
5
4
3
2
1
.Ed
.Pp
Print with comma separator:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ seq -s ", " 3
1, 2, 3
.Ed
.Pp
Print with zero-padded equal width:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ seq -w 1 10
01
02
03
04
05
06
07
08
09
10
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jot 1 ,
.Xr printf 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is not specified by POSIX.
It is compatible with the GNU coreutils
.Nm
command.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
